      <div>
        <h2>A documented risk on two fronts for event medical support</h2>
        <p class="partner-section__lead">Exertional heat stroke (EHS) affects an event medical setup in two distinct ways. First, the participants themselves, exposed to prolonged effort in heat, sometimes over several hours of racing. Second, the volunteers and medical staff at the advanced medical post, working in the same location and the same conditions.</p>
        <p class="partner-section__lead">For a complete breakdown, read our case review on <a href="https://kollder.com/blog/en/2026-05-26-hyrox-lyon-death-heat-stroke-cooling-protocol/" class="inline-link">the Lyon Hyrox fatality</a> and our <a href="https://kollder.com/blog/en/2026-05-21-trail-marathon-hyrox-2026-heat-stroke-protocol/" class="inline-link">heat stroke protocol for event medical teams</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="partner-narrative__side">
        <div class="partner-callout">
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">50%+</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">EHS mortality without proper cooling</div>
          </div>
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">30 min</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">Critical window to bring core temperature below 40°C</div>
          </div>
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">→ 0</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">Mortality with a correctly applied immersion protocol</div>
          </div>
        </div>
