</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 : LE PROTOCOLE DE REFERENCE -->
<section class="partner-section partner-section--alt">
  <div class="partner-section__inner">
    <h2>Cool First, Transport Second: the reference protocol</h2>
    <p class="partner-section__lead">The foundational rule validated by the ACSM and the International Olympic Committee applies right at the finish line or the aid station: never evacuate a runner with exertional heat stroke before cooling them. Cold water immersion remains, to date, the only method with a cooling rate that is clinically fast enough.</p>

    <div class="partner-stats">
      <div class="partner-stat">
        <div class="partner-stat__figure">×10</div>
        <div class="partner-stat__label">cooling rate</div>
        <p>Whole-body cold water immersion cools at 0.35°C per minute, compared to roughly 0.03°C per minute for wet towels or ice packs. Source: Casa DJ et al., Exercise and Sport Sciences Reviews, 2007.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="partner-stat">
        <div class="partner-stat__figure">100%</div>
        <div class="partner-stat__label">documented survival</div>
        <p>Across 401 exertional heat stroke cases treated with immediate cold water immersion, survival is 100% when core temperature drops below 40°C within 30 minutes. Source: Korey Stringer Institute, Douglas Casa, UConn.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="partner-stat">
        <div class="partner-stat__figure">Regulatory</div>
        <div class="partner-stat__label">momentum is building</div>
        <p>OSHA's proposed heat injury and illness prevention standard is pushing US event organizers toward documented heat response equipment. France has already made this mandatory for sporting event organizers under Décret n°2025-482.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="partner-cta-row" style="margin-top:44px;">
      <a href="https://kollder.com/blog/en/2026-05-25-cool-first-transport-second-protocol-complete-guide/" class="partner-btn partner-btn--outline">Read the complete protocol</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

