<section class="partner-section">
  <div class="partner-section__inner partner-section--narrow">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="partner-faq">

      <details>
        <summary>How do you treat exertional heat stroke at an event medical post?</summary>
        <p>The reference treatment, validated by the ACSM and the IOC, is whole-body cold water immersion started immediately at the medical post, before any transport. This is the Cool First, Transport Second protocol: cooling takes priority over evacuation until the athlete's core temperature drops below 38.6°C.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What should you do for exertional heat stroke at a race before advanced medical support arrives?</summary>
        <p>Immerse the runner in cold water up to the shoulders immediately and alert EMS in parallel. According to the Korey Stringer Institute, survival reaches 100% across more than 400 documented cases when core temperature drops below 40°C within 30 minutes of initial treatment.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How long does it take to deploy an emergency cooling tub at an event?</summary>
        <p>An emergency cooling tub designed for operational use should be deployable by one person, with no tools, in under 2 minutes. This is a decisive requirement at a medical post, where the equipment needs to be operational as soon as the event opens.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What criteria matter when choosing cooling equipment for event medical support?</summary>
        <p>Four criteria matter most: single-operator deployment speed, portability to the medical post with no dedicated vehicle required, an easy-to-disinfect material such as food-grade stainless steel, and 360-degree patient access for clinical monitoring throughout immersion.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does a cooling tub fit at a race medical post?</summary>
        <p>Yes, provided it is designed for field use: folded down into a compact, lightweight format, it can be carried on foot, as a backpack, or rolled to the medical post, with no dedicated vehicle needed.</p>
      </details>

    </div>

